/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Glossary (paraphrased from Appendix A and the body of the report).
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const GLOSSARY = [
  ["Adversarial attack", "An intentional strategy that crafts or alters inputs, context or training data so that the system acts against its objectives or safety constraints, for example by disclosing confidential data or executing prohibited instructions.", "§5.3.2"],
  ["Adversarial testing", "A systematic way to assess how a model behaves when exposed to malicious or unintentionally harmful inputs.", "App. A"],
  ["AI agent", "A software system that uses GenAI to pursue goals autonomously on behalf of users. It can plan, keep context in memory, make decisions, use predefined tools and adapt its behaviour.", "§5.2.4"],
  ["AI gateway", "A centralised control point between users and models that logs requests and responses, helps detect leakage and anomalous use, and centralises API access to reduce provider dependency.", "§5.3.4.3"],
  ["AI model vs AI system", "Following the IAA (2025), the model is the inferential core that turns inputs into outputs; the system is the full operational layer around it, including infrastructure and governance. Many risks (bias, hallucination, opacity) start in the model.", "App. A"],
  ["AI system (AIS)", "Definitions vary by text (NAIC Model Bulletin, NY Circular Letter No. 7, TRAIGA, EU AI Act). The common core is a machine-based system that infers from inputs how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions, with varying autonomy.", "App. A"],
  ["AIS Program", "The written programme for responsible use of AI systems recommended by the NAIC Model Bulletin, covering governance, risk management, internal audit and lifecycle controls.", "§2.1"],
  ["Alignment", "Steering an AI model toward an intended behaviour, ethical principles or preferences, through prompts, model adaptation or system design.", "§5.2"],
  ["Alignment faking", "A large model's tendency to appear aligned with a training objective while monitored, to avoid having its behaviour changed, and to revert when unmonitored.", "App. C.2.2"],
  ["Chain-of-Thought (CoT)", "A prompting technique that asks the model to set out intermediate reasoning steps before its final answer. It improves interpretability and can help debugging.", "§5.2.4.1"],
  ["Code of practice", "Under the EU AI Act, a voluntary tool prepared by independent experts in a multi-stakeholder process to help providers of general-purpose AI comply. Downstream providers can rely on it.", "App. A"],
  ["Consequential decision", "Under Colorado SB26-189, a decision about a consumer's access to, eligibility for, or material terms of a covered domain, which includes insurance underwriting, pricing, coverage and claims.", "App. A"],
  ["Context window", "The maximum number of tokens a model can take in at once. Chat history uses it up, so older content is eventually forgotten.", "§5.1.4.1"],
  ["COSTAR", "A prompt structure: Context, Objective, Style, Tone, Audience, Response. Mostly used for system prompts.", "§5.2.2.2"],
  ["Data drift", "When production data differs from the data the system was trained or aligned on. Sub-types: concept drift (meanings or domain change) and statistical drift (how requests are phrased changes).", "§5.3.3"],
  ["Data poisoning", "Contaminating training data with malicious content to change model outputs. One study found that replacing 0.001% of training tokens with medical misinformation produced harmful outputs.", "§5.3.2"],
  ["Data sheet", "A standard document describing a dataset: motivation, composition, collection, preprocessing, intended uses, distribution and maintenance.", "App. A"],
  ["Deployer", "Broadly, the organisation using an AI system under its own authority. Under the EU AI Act, deployers of high-risk systems carry obligations such as human oversight, monitoring and, in some areas, a fundamental rights impact assessment.", "§2.3"],
  ["Developer / Provider", "The party that develops an AI system, or has it developed, and places it on the market under its own name. Materially modifying a foundation model (e.g., fine-tuning it) and distributing the result can make an insurer a provider.", "§4.2"],
  ["ECDIS", "External Consumer Data and Information Sources: non-traditional data such as credit scores, social media habits, purchasing habits, IoT or biometric data, used to supplement or replace traditional underwriting factors.", "App. A"],
  ["Embedding", "A dense numeric vector representing a token or text so that similar meanings sit close together (e.g., King − Man + Woman ≈ Queen).", "§5.1.1.2"],
  ["Explainability", "The ability to produce post hoc explanations of a non-transparent model's behaviour using auxiliary methods. It can be local (one output) or global (overall behaviour).", "App. D.2"],
  ["Fact sheet", "A concise summary of an AI service's purpose, performance, fairness metrics, limits and intended deployment conditions.", "App. A"],
  ["Few-shot / zero-shot prompting", "Including several examples (few-shot), one example (one-shot) or none (zero-shot) in the prompt to show the model how to answer.", "§5.2.2.3"],
  ["Fine-tuning", "A further training pass on a pre-trained model with task-specific data, which adjusts its parameters. Variants include supervised fine-tuning, RLHF and parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA.", "§5.2.3.3"],
  ["General-purpose AI system (GPAIS)", "Under the EU AI Act, a model trained at scale that shows significant generality and can perform a wide range of distinct tasks, and can be integrated into many downstream systems.", "App. A"],
  ["Hallucination", "Output that appears plausible and confident but is false or unverifiable. Factual hallucinations contradict real-world facts; faithfulness hallucinations contradict the input or themselves.", "§5.1.3.1"],
  ["High-risk AI system (HRAIS)", "Under the EU AI Act, a system in an Annex III area that may significantly affect people's rights, such as pricing and risk assessment for individuals in life and health insurance.", "§2.3"],
  ["Human-in / on / out-of-the-loop", "In: the system waits for explicit human approval at key points. On: it runs autonomously while humans monitor and can intervene. Out: humans only set up, review periodically and govern.", "§5.2.6"],
  ["LLM-as-a-judge", "Using one LLM to evaluate another's outputs, by direct assessment (with or without a reference) or pairwise comparison. It scales, but can show verbosity, self-enhancement and positional biases.", "App. D.1"],
  ["LoRA", "Low-Rank Adaptation: freezes the pre-trained weights and trains small low-rank matrices, typically under 1% of parameters.", "App. C.2.1"],
  ["Model card", "A short document accompanying a trained model: intended use, performance across conditions and groups, training data, limitations and ethical considerations.", "App. A"],
  ["Model Context Protocol (MCP)", "An open protocol introduced by Anthropic in 2024 that standardises how agents connect to tools and data. It cuts integrations from M × N to M + N and keeps tool use traceable and permissioned.", "§5.2.5"],
  ["Model governance", "Protecting the organisation from model risk: decisions taken on unreliable, inaccurate or unstable outputs.", "App. A"],
  ["Multi-agent system (MAS)", "Several agents collaborating, organised as independent, centralised, decentralised or hybrid orchestration.", "§5.2.4.2"],
  ["Non-public information (NPI)", "Personally identifiable financial information provided to or obtained by a financial institution that is not publicly available (GLBA).", "§2.2"],
  ["Orchestration", "The control layer that directs agents and tools toward a goal, managing sequencing, state, guardrails and user feedback.", "App. A"],
  ["Privacy-enhancing technologies (PETs)", "Technical solutions that protect personal data by minimising collection and exposure, strengthening security and giving individuals more control.", "App. A"],
  ["Profiling", "Automated processing of personal data to evaluate or predict aspects of a person such as health, economic situation or behaviour. Some US state laws give an opt-out right where it supports solely automated decisions with significant effects.", "App. A"],
  ["Prompt injection", "An attack that inserts crafted instructions into a model's input to override its legitimate instructions. It can be direct (typed) or indirect (hidden in an uploaded file).", "§5.3.2"],
  ["Prompt sensitivity", "The tendency of LLMs to respond differently, sometimes significantly, to small changes in how a prompt is phrased.", "§5.1.3.3"],
  ["RAG", "Retrieval-augmented generation: retrieves relevant passages from an external knowledge base at query time and adds them to the prompt, without changing the model.", "§5.2.3.2"],
  ["RAGAS", "A framework for evaluating RAG systems with metrics such as faithfulness, answer relevance and context relevance, computed with the help of an external LLM.", "App. C.1.2"],
  ["ReAct", "An agent pattern that loops through Reason, Act and Observe until it gives a final answer.", "§5.2.4.1"],
  ["Red teaming", "Simulating worst-case use and adversarial attacks to stress-test safeguards, before deployment and periodically afterwards.", "§5.3.4.5"],
  ["RLHF", "Reinforcement learning from human feedback: humans rank outputs, a reward model learns from the rankings, and the LLM is tuned toward higher-reward responses.", "§5.2.3.3"],
  ["Risk register", "A structured log of identified risks with likelihood, severity, mitigation and owner. It comes from project and risk management and applies to AI governance.", "App. A"],
  ["Safety vs security", "Safety protects people from harm caused by the system's outputs. Security protects the system from external threats and malicious actors.", "App. A"],
  ["System card", "A document describing the entire AI system: how the model is integrated, deployment context, safety evaluations, risk mitigations and real-world testing.", "App. A"],
  ["System prompt / user prompt", "The system prompt is set by the developer, applies to every interaction and is usually invisible to users. The user prompt is the specific request typed by the user.", "§5.2.2.1"],
  ["Temperature, Top-P, Top-K", "Generation parameters. Temperature reshapes token probabilities (low = predictable); Top-P keeps tokens up to a cumulative probability; Top-K keeps the K most likely tokens.", "§5.1.4"],
  ["Toxicity", "Generation of offensive, discriminatory, hateful or otherwise harmful content, whether prompted or not.", "App. A"],
  ["Transformer", "The 2017 neural architecture behind modern LLMs. Its attention mechanism weighs every token against all others, which captures context such as 'river bank' versus 'bank loan'.", "§5.1.1.3"]
];

